            <note corresp="#d152-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d152-f1-content">
                <p>Source: National Archives and Records Administration, RG 59, S/AL Files: Lot 67 D 2. Top
                    Secret. An attached December 8 covering memorandum from Thompson to Ambassador Bohlen indicates that the letter should be
                    opened only by the Secretary.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d152-f2" n="2" xml:id="d152-f2-content">
                <p>See <ref target="#d148-f8">footnote 8, Document 148</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d152-f3" n="3" xml:id="d152-f3-content">
                <p>No other record of this meeting has been found.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d152-f4" n="4" xml:id="d152-f4-content">
                <p>See <ref target="#d150-f3">footnote 3, Document 150</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d152-f5" n="5" xml:id="d152-f5-content">
                <p>Regarding Thompson’s December 6
                    conversation with Dobrynin, see <ref
                        target="frus1964-68v11#d168fn2">
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Foreign Relations</hi>, 1964–1968, vol. XI, Document
                        168, footnote 2</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d152-f6" n="6" xml:id="d152-f6-content">
                <p>The words “by the President” were typed in the margin and inserted in the text at
                    this point. Regarding the President’s authorization, see <ref
                        target="frus1964-68v11#d168fn4">ibid., vol. XI, Document 168, footnote
                        4</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d152-f7" n="7" xml:id="d152-f7-content">
                <p>Not attached.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d152-f8" n="8" xml:id="d152-f8-content">
